{{ robot.name }} runs the **universal BOTFORGE firmware**: one firmware image
shared by every robot, told what it is by a small config file. The config —
which motors, sensors and LEDs are on which pins — was generated from this
robot's design, so there is nothing to program and nothing to edit. Flashing
happens right in your browser; no toolchains, no drivers to hunt down.

Use a Chromium-based browser (Chrome or Edge) on a laptop or desktop — browser
flashing uses WebSerial, which phones and Safari don't support.

## Step 1 — Plug in over USB

Connect the robot's brain board to your computer with a **data-capable USB-C
cable**. If the board offers two ports, use the one labelled **USB** (not
UART). The power switch can stay off — the board runs from USB while flashing.

## Step 2 — Hold BOOT

Hold down the board's **BOOT** button, tap **RESET** (or re-plug the cable
while still holding BOOT), then release. This puts the chip in download mode
so the browser is allowed to write firmware to it.

## Step 3 — Flash from the site

Open the [/flash](/flash/) page on the site, pick **{{ robot.name }}**, and
click **Flash**. Choose the board's serial port in the browser prompt and
wait for the progress bar — about a minute. The robot reboots by itself when
flashing finishes.

That's it. The firmware and this robot's config are now on the board —
continue to [first run](./first-run/) to get it on your Wi-Fi.
